# ExplainTool screen contract — the tool owns the terminal's rows while it is up,
# and hands them back cleanly on ctrl-d.
#
#   open:  type a code, tab opens the interactive explain table
#   nav:   every key redraws in place — the old pointer row must not survive
#   exit:  ctrl-d hands the terminal back; the screen repaints itself from its
#          own content (a full repaint — boot text re-emitted), the final frame
#          is the up-state one, and no stale frame lingers beside it
#
#   bin/metatron-docker build console --steps bin/test/explain-tool.steps

WAIT_CONSOLE

# --- the table opens on tab ---
MARK open
TYPE [1,2,3].map(x=>x+1)
SLEEP 0.6
KEY tab
WAIT c_dom

# --- down: the new frame replaces the old one in place ---
MARK down
KEY down
WAIT_SINCE down ctrl-d:back
SILENT down ">start" 2

# --- up: and back again, one frame at a time ---
MARK up
KEY up
WAIT_SINCE up ctrl-d:back
SILENT up ">map" 2

# --- ctrl-d: the tool leaves exactly one (last) frame in the transcript ---
MARK exit
KEY ctrl+d
# a full repaint from content re-emits the boot banner; the exit is the moment
# the screen reasserts ownership of what is on screen
WAIT_SINCE exit metatron successfully booted 5
WAIT_SINCE exit mtron>
# the session ended on the up-state frame: its down-state twin must not linger
SILENT exit ">map" 2

SLEEP 1
